   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 563 of the laws of 2008, amending the education law and the public health law, relating to immunizing agents to be administered to adults by pharmacists, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S3807",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3807-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §8, Chap 563 of 2008",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3807\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend chapter 563 of the laws of 2008, amending the education law and\nthe public health law, relating to immunizing agents to be\nadministered to adults by pharmacists, in relation to the effectiveness\nthereof\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo remove the sunset provision of chapter 563 of the laws of 2008\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 amends the effective date provision of chapter 563 of the\nlaws of 2008 to remove the sunset\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nPresently, all provisions of chapter 563 of 2008 are set to expire on\nMarch 31, 2012.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThis bill is intended to improve health results in New York State, by\nallowing pharmacists to continue to administer vaccinations. Chapter\n563 of 2008 allows pharmacists, after receiving proper training and\ncertification by the Department of Education, to administer vaccines\nfor flu and pneumococcal to adults. This authority is set to expire\nin March of 2012. This bill extends this authority - which is granted\nin 48 other states - indefinitely.\n\nIn the two years since NYS provided such authority to pharmacists,\nimmunization rates have risen dramatically. Since implementation in\nthe fall of 2008, more than 5,000 NYS licensed pharmacists completed\ncertification requirements and estimates suggest that pharmacists have\nadministered more than one million immunizations in New York in the\npast twelve months.\n\nClearly, this initiative has been successful by any measure and the\nsunset should therefore be removed.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n\n",
